---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
const calendly = 'https://calendly.com/contact-advised-seo/30min?back=1';
const jsonLd = JSON.stringify({
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      "headline": "Reclaim the demand marketplaces take from you",
      "description": "Amazon captures the demand your brand creates. Here's the real cost, the mechanism, and how to take back your direct sales without a head-on war.",
      "inLanguage": "en",
      "author": { "@type": "Person", "name": "Francis Ndimba", "url": "https://www.afrirank.fr/en/about/" },
      "publisher": { "@id": "https://www.afrirank.fr/#afrirank" },
      "mainEntityOfPage": "https://www.afrirank.fr/en/reclaim-demand-from-marketplaces/",
      "datePublished": "2026-07-16",
      "dateModified": "2026-07-17",
      "about": [
        { "@type": "Organization", "name": "Amazon", "sameAs": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amazon_(company)" },
        { "@type": "Thing", "name": "Online marketplaces" },
        { "@type": "Thing", "name": "Direct-to-consumer sales" },
        { "@type": "Thing", "name": "E-commerce SEO" }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      "itemListElement": [
        { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 1, "name": "Home", "item": "https://www.afrirank.fr/en/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 2, "name": "SEO for African & Black-owned brands", "item": "https://www.afrirank.fr/en/seo-african-heritage-brands/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 3, "name": "Reclaim the demand marketplaces capture", "item": "https://www.afrirank.fr/en/reclaim-demand-from-marketplaces/" }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      "inLanguage": "en",
      "mainEntity": [
        { "@type": "Question", "name": "Do I have to leave Amazon and the marketplaces?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Not necessarily. They can stay a discovery or volume channel. The goal isn't to cut everything off, but to stop handing them the demand you create yourself — especially on your brand." } },
        { "@type": "Question", "name": "Why does a marketplace rank on my own brand name?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Because its domain is old, huge and technically very optimised. Google judges it trustworthy, so it ranks it — even on your name. It's a technical gap, and on branded queries it's very much closeable." } },
        { "@type": "Question", "name": "How much revenue actually goes to the marketplaces?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Impossible to say without looking at your data: Search Console, analytics, and your own marketplace sales. That's exactly what a diagnostic quantifies, in order of magnitude." } },
        { "@type": "Question", "name": "Why not fight on every generic query?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Because taking on a domain of several million pages across the whole niche costs a lot and returns little. We pick the precise queries where your legitimacy beats their catalogue." } },
        { "@type": "Question", "name": "Is a direct sale really worth more than a marketplace sale?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Yes, and the gap goes beyond the commission: full margin, the customer's email, the chance to build loyalty and bring them back, control of the experience. A direct sale builds your asset; a marketplace sale builds theirs." } }
      ]
    }
  ]
});
---
<Base
  title="Reclaim the demand marketplaces capture | AfriRank"
  description="Amazon captures the demand your brand creates. Here's the real cost, the mechanism, and how to take back your direct sales without a head-on war."
  lang="en"
  frPath="/reprendre-demande-marketplaces/"
  enPath="/en/reclaim-demand-from-marketplaces/"
  jsonLd={jsonLd}>

<section class="hero hero--page wrap">
  <div class="hero-top">
    <nav class="breadcrumb reveal" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/en/">Home</a><span class="sep">›</span>
      <a href="/en/seo-african-heritage-brands/">SEO for Black-owned brands</a><span class="sep">›</span>
      <span>Reclaim the demand marketplaces capture</span>
    </nav>
    <span class="hero-eyebrow reveal"><img src="/images/icon-orange.png" alt="" /> Guide · Territory 2</span>
    <h1 class="reveal">Reclaim the demand <em>marketplaces take from you</em>.</h1>
    <p class="hero-sub reveal">You built the demand. And when it's time to buy, some of your customers type your name, see Amazon or a marketplace at the top, click, and buy there. The product sold — just not from you.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="sec" style="padding-top:clamp(24px,4vw,48px)">
  <div class="wrap article reveal">
    <p class="lead">You paid for the awareness, the marketplace banked the conversion. You did the demand work, it took the margin and, along the way, the customer data. This isn't an awareness problem. It's a capture problem. And capture can be taken back.</p>

    <h2>The mechanism: how a marketplace captures your demand</h2>
    <p>A marketplace doesn't create your demand. It intercepts it. Two channels.</p>
    <h3>Channel 1: branded demand</h3>
    <p>Someone searches your name, or your name + a product. If the marketplace ranks on those queries — and it often does, because its domain is colossal — it captures a click that was meant for you. This is the most expensive capture for you, because it's hot demand, already decided, that you generated yourself.</p>
    <h3>Channel 2: generic queries</h3>
    <p>"castor oil for hair growth", "foundation for deep skin tones", "African &amp; Caribbean grocery online". The searcher isn't looking for you yet — they're looking for a solution. The marketplace dominates these queries through sheer page count and slots itself between the need and you. You become a supplier in its catalogue, not a brand in the customer's mind. The more generics it captures, the more reviews it stacks up on your products, the more it then ranks on your brand. The loop closes.</p>

    <h2>The real cost (beyond the commission)</h2>
    <h3>The margin</h3>
    <p>Every sale that runs through a marketplace means a commission taken plus, often, pressure on price. The same sale direct leaves you the full margin. At volume, the gap is massive and compounds month after month.</p>
    <h3>The customer data</h3>
    <p>This is the most underrated cost. A direct sale gives you an email, buying behaviour, the chance to build loyalty. A marketplace sale gives you an order number. The customer belongs to the platform, not to you.</p>
    <h3>The dependence</h3>
    <p>The larger the share of your revenue running through a marketplace, the more power it holds over you: terms, ranking, fees, visibility. The day the rules change, you take the hit.</p>
    <h3>The erasure of the brand</h3>
    <p>On a marketplace, you're one listing among thousands, in the platform's template. For an African or Black-owned brand whose strength is community connection and identity, that's a strategic impoverishment, not just a financial one.</p>

    <h2>The reclaim logic: choosing your battles</h2>
    <p>The trap to avoid: trying to take everything back at once. Fighting a marketplace head-on across every generic means taking on a domain of several million pages. Smart reclaiming picks the winnable battles, in this order.</p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Branded demand.</strong> On your own name, the marketplace has no decisive advantage. Taking back the No.1 spot on "[brand]" recovers the hottest demand. <a href="/en/own-your-branded-serp/">See how to own your branded SERP.</a></li>
      <li><strong>Niche generics where you're legitimate.</strong> Not "hair oil" in general, but the precise queries where your product is exactly the right answer. You win because you're more relevant than a catalogue.</li>
      <li><strong>Conversion to direct.</strong> Once you've won the click back, buying direct has to be the obvious choice: better offer, brand experience, reasons to come back.</li>
    </ol>

    <blockquote><p>The Diagnostic shows the what and the how much. The how — the query-by-query, page-by-page reclaim — belongs to the engagement.</p></blockquote>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="sec" style="padding-top:0">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="sec-head reveal"><span class="kicker">Frequently asked</span><h2 class="sec-title">Your questions, my answers.</h2></div>
    <div class="faq">
      <div class="faq-item reveal"><button class="faq-q">Do I have to leave Amazon and the marketplaces? <span class="pm"></span></button><div class="faq-a"><p>Not necessarily. They can stay a discovery or volume channel. The goal isn't to cut everything off, but to stop handing them the demand you create yourself — especially on your brand. We rebalance toward direct.</p></div></div>
      <div class="faq-item reveal"><button class="faq-q">Why does a marketplace rank on my own brand name? <span class="pm"></span></button><div class="faq-a"><p>Because its domain is old, huge and technically very optimised. Google judges it trustworthy, so it ranks it — even on your name. On branded queries, that gap is very much closeable.</p></div></div>
      <div class="faq-item reveal"><button class="faq-q">How much revenue actually goes to the marketplaces? <span class="pm"></span></button><div class="faq-a"><p>Impossible to say without looking at your data: Search Console, analytics, and your marketplace sales. That's exactly what a diagnostic quantifies, in order of magnitude, rather than throwing out a generic percentage.</p></div></div>
      <div class="faq-item reveal"><button class="faq-q">Why not fight on every generic query? <span class="pm"></span></button><div class="faq-a"><p>Because taking on a domain of several million pages across the whole niche costs a lot and returns little. We pick the precise queries where your legitimacy beats their catalogue. Fewer fronts, more wins.</p></div></div>
      <div class="faq-item reveal"><button class="faq-q">Is a direct sale really worth more? <span class="pm"></span></button><div class="faq-a"><p>Yes, and the gap goes beyond the commission: full margin, the customer's email, the chance to build loyalty and bring them back, control of the experience. A direct sale builds your asset; a marketplace sale builds theirs.</p></div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="sec" style="padding-top:0">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="links-more reveal">
      <h2>Go further</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="/en/seo-african-heritage-brands/">SEO strategy for African & Black-owned brands</a></li>
        <li><a href="/en/recover-revenue-from-resellers/">Recover the revenue your resellers capture</a></li>
        <li><a href="/en/marketplace-vs-brand-site/">Marketplace vs brand site: who captures the traffic?</a></li>
        <li><a href="/en/why-resellers-outrank-your-brand/">Why my resellers outrank my brand</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="sec">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="cta-band reveal">
      <img src="/images/icon-cream.png" class="icon-watermark" alt="" />
      <h2>How much of your demand goes to the marketplaces?</h2>
      <p>The Diagnostic lays out the queries where a marketplace edges you out, the order of magnitude of the revenue flowing elsewhere, and the winnable battles ranked by effort/reward.</p>
      <div class="cta-actions">
        <a href={calendly} target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="btn btn--orange">Book the Diagnostic
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M5 12h14M13 5l7 7-7 7"/></svg>
        </a>
        <a href="/en/seo-diagnostic/" class="btn btn--cream">Learn more</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
</Base>
